132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Multithreading is an important concept in modern operating systems that allows a single process to perform multiple tasks concurrently. A thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process, and multiple threads can share the same memory and resources. This makes multithreading useful for applications that need better performance, responsiveness, and efficient CPU utilization.Linux provides strong support for multithreaded programming through the POSIX Threads \(Pthreads\) library. Functions such as pthread_create\(\), pthread_join\(\), and pthread_exit\(\) are used to create, manage, and terminate threads.When multiple threads access a common resource, problems such as race conditions can occur. To avoid these problems, the project uses mutexes. A mutex allows only one thread at a time to access a critical section of code. This ensures that shared data is modified safely and consistently.The project therefore focuses on implementing a Linux application that demonstrates thread creation, concurrent execution, synchronization using mutexes, critical-section protection, and safe resource management.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
